## Category 1: Clinical Foundation — Singapore Context

### 1.1 Ministry of Health Singapore (2024). National Mental Health and Well-being Strategy — Youth Mental Health Report.

**Citation:** Ministry of Health Singapore. (2024). Whole-of-Society Efforts to Support Mental Health and Well-being of Youths in Singapore. MOH.

**Summary:** This official MOH report establishes the epidemiological baseline for youth mental health in Singapore. Key findings include 1 in 4 young adults (18–29) reporting poor mental health in 2024 — the highest rate across all age groups. The report documents the reach of existing services: CHAT has supported 7,600+ youths, CREST-Youth has reached 98,000+ individuals, and 88.7% of young adults prefer online over face-to-face help-seeking. The report identifies early identification and upstream prevention as the primary gaps in the current system.

**Relevance to Pipeline:** Provides the primary justification for this project. The 88.7% online preference statistic directly motivates the use of digital/NLP-based detection rather than waiting for clinic-based identification. The 1 in 4 statistic establishes the scale of unmet need.

**Key Number:** 1 in 4 young adults reported poor mental health (2024) — highest across all age groups.

