## Category 3: Multilingual AI — Google DeepMind Research

### 3.1 Google DeepMind (2025). Prompting with Phonemes: Enhancing LLM Multilinguality for non-Latin Scripts.

**Citation:** Google DeepMind. (2025, April 29). Prompting with Phonemes: Enhancing LLM Multilinguality for non-Latin Scripts.

**Summary:** This paper addresses a fundamental limitation of current LLMs: they perform significantly worse on non-Latin script languages (including Chinese, Japanese, Korean) compared to English. The authors demonstrate that phoneme-based prompting strategies substantially improve LLM performance on non-Latin languages. The paper provides quantitative evidence that the gap between English and Chinese LLM performance is systematic and architecture-level — not simply a data quantity issue.

**Relevance to Pipeline:** Provides technical evidence for why English-trained LLMs systematically underperform on Chinese adolescent mental health text — the core problem this pipeline addresses. Supports the decision to use Chinese MentalBERT rather than prompting a general English-first LLM.

**Key Finding:** Non-Latin script languages suffer systematic, architecture-level performance gaps in current LLMs.

---

### 3.2 Google DeepMind (2025). EmbeddingGemma: Powerful and Lightweight Text Representations.

**Citation:** Google DeepMind. (2025, September 24). EmbeddingGemma: Powerful and Lightweight Text Representations.

**Summary:** EmbeddingGemma introduces lightweight text embedding models derived from the Gemma architecture, optimised for high-quality multilingual text representations. The models achieve strong performance on multilingual benchmarks while remaining computationally efficient. The paper demonstrates particular strength on semantic similarity and classification tasks — both central to mental health text analysis.

**Relevance to Pipeline:** EmbeddingGemma-style lightweight text representations are relevant to Tier 2 (Risk Scoring) feature extraction. For deployment in resource-constrained Singapore school settings, lightweight embedding models that maintain multilingual performance are preferable to full fine-tuned transformers.

**Key Finding:** Lightweight multilingual embeddings can match large model performance on classification tasks.

---

### 3.3 Google DeepMind (2024). Tx-LLM: A Large Language Model for Therapeutics.

**Citation:** Google DeepMind. (2024, June 10). Tx-LLM: A Large Language Model for Therapeutics.

**Summary:** Tx-LLM demonstrates that LLMs can be successfully adapted for clinical and therapeutic applications, establishing a methodological precedent for domain-specific LLM fine-tuning in high-stakes clinical contexts. The paper includes governance and safety frameworks for responsible clinical AI deployment. The authors argue that domain-specific fine-tuning dramatically outperforms general-purpose LLMs in clinical settings.

**Relevance to Pipeline:** Provides precedent for the clinical AI approach in this pipeline. The argument that domain-specific fine-tuning outperforms general LLMs in clinical contexts supports the use of Chinese MentalBERT over general-purpose multilingual models.

**Key Finding:** Domain-specific LLM fine-tuning dramatically outperforms general-purpose models in clinical settings.

