## Category 4: Cognitive Science Foundation

### 4.1 Eckstein, M.K., Summerfield, C., Daw, N.D., & Miller, K.J. (2026). Hybrid neural-cognitive models reveal how memory shapes human reward learning.

**Citation:** Eckstein, M.K., Summerfield, C., Daw, N.D., & Miller, K.J. (2026). Hybrid neural-cognitive models reveal how memory shapes human reward learning. Nature Human Behaviour. DOI: 10.1038/s41562-025-02324-0

**Summary:** Published in Nature Human Behaviour by Google DeepMind and Princeton researchers, this paper demonstrates that human behaviour cannot be adequately modelled by simple reinforcement learning algorithms. Using a hybrid neural-cognitive modelling approach (n=862 participants, 617,871 valid trials), the authors show that successful models require flexible memory variables tracking reward history at multiple timescales. The winning Memory-ANN model achieves 68.3% predictive accuracy vs 60.6% for the best classic RL model.

**Relevance to Pipeline:** Informs Tier 3 (Temporal Forecasting) design. The finding that human behaviour encodes risk history at multiple timescales supports LSTM-based 7-day trajectory prediction. The Memory-ANN architecture — separating memory variables from choice variables — provides theoretical basis for separating historical context features from immediate distress signals in the temporal layer.

**Key Finding:** Human behaviour encodes reward/risk history at multiple timescales — supporting LSTM temporal modelling over point-in-time assessment.

